      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from a bend, or “bow,” in the Merrimack River that runs along its northeastern border.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1874 the Concord Railroad ran along Bow’s eastern edge, later becoming part of the New England Southern Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In September 2019, climate activists staged a protest in Bow against the Merrimack Station coal plant, one of New England’s last coal‑fired power stations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
